          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Homer's Identity</strong><br>\nHomer is traditionally considered the author of the two great epics of ancient Greece, the \"Iliad\" and the \"Odyssey.\" The dates of his life are not certain, but he is thought to have lived during the 8th century BCE. There is much debate over whether Homer was a historical individual or a symbolic name representing a group of poets who passed down these epic poems through an oral tradition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Iliad</strong><br>\nThe \"Iliad\" is set during the Trojan War and focuses on the Greek hero Achilles. It is known for its depiction of the siege of Troy and the famous characters, including Hector and Paris. The poem reflects the values and society of ancient Greece, such as the concept of heroism and the role of the gods in human affairs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Odyssey</strong><br>\nThe \"Odyssey\" tells the story of Odysseus, King of Ithaca, and his journey home after the fall of Troy. It is celebrated for its adventure narrative, including encounters with the Cyclops, Circe, and the Sirens. The poem also explores themes of loyalty, perseverance, and the struggle of Odysseus to reclaim his kingdom.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
