          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Oral Tradition</strong><br>\nHomer's epics were part of an oral tradition long before they were written down. Bards, like Homer, would perform these stories with the help of memory aids such as repetition and rhythm. This oral tradition allowed the poems to be transmitted across generations before literacy became widespread.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Homeric Question</strong><br>\nScholars have debated the \"Homeric Question,\" which concerns the authorship of the \"Iliad\" and the \"Odyssey.\" Some argue that the poems were the work of a single genius, while others believe they are the result of a collective process involving many contributors over time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Homeric Language</strong><br>\nThe language of Homer's poetry is Ancient Greek, specifically an archaic form mixed with different dialects. This blend reflects the transition from the oral to the written tradition and the regional variations in the Greek language at the time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
