          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Homeric Influence</strong><br>\nHomer's influence on Western literature is profound. His epics provided a foundation for Greek education and were considered essential learning for well-rounded individuals. His work influenced countless authors and persists in the modern arts and literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Homeric Society</strong><br>\nThe society depicted in Homer's epics is one that values honor, bravery, and the warrior code. The poems provide insight into the political, social, and religious life of early Greece, showcasing the importance of hospitality, respect for the gods, and the social structure of the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Historicity of Troy</strong><br>\nThe city of Troy, central to the \"Iliad,\" was long considered mythical until archaeologists discovered ruins in modern-day Turkey. This discovery has lent some historical credibility to the Trojan War, though the extent to which Homer's account reflects historical events remains uncertain.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
